comments.content:
`requireRoleAccess` is a UI-oriented helper: on unauthenticated access it calls `redirectToLogin` and on role mismatch it calls `redirect(getDashboardPathForRole(...))` — both rely on Next.js `redirect()` throwing a navigation error. This is safe only because those helper functions are currently declared `: never`; nothing in this route guarantees that contract. If either ever returns instead of throwing, `user` becomes `undefined` and the subsequent `user.id` access throws a TypeError after the asset state has already been mutated. Consider resolving the user defensively, e.g. verify the returned value carries an id before proceeding:

```ts
const user = await requireRoleAccess("en", ["ADMIN"]);
if (!user?.id) return NextResponse.json({ error: "Forbidden." }, { status: 403 });
```

`params.id` is forwarded to the service without any validation. An empty or whitespace-only id (e.g. `/api/admin/assets/%20/quarantine`) bypasses the Zod schema entirely and reaches the database lookup, producing a generic 404/500 rather than a 400 with a clear message. Validate and trim the path parameter before calling the service:

```ts
const assetId = params.id?.trim();
if (!assetId) return NextResponse.json({ error: "Asset id is required." }, { status: 400 });
```

